From 31109f7f80bcb9532010ba2ab4b8fbdf3a46f844 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Obialor <danielobialor121@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 16:49:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Skeleton loaders, Chinwe Nnanna on the team, address
 corrected to Garrison
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Address. The Business Profile reads "6 Aguma street, Garrison" — the site read
"6 Chief Aguma St, Nkpogu" in 23 places: three meta descriptions per page, the
JSON-LD streetAddress, the announcement bar, the hero eyebrow, the contact block,
both footers, the pickup radio on checkout, the printed receipt and the map embed.
NAP has to read identically on the site, in the schema and on the profile, so all
of it moved, including the two generators. The map embed now searches the business
name as well as the street, so the pin lands on the listing rather than the road.

Team. Chinaza Justin (Network Specialist) is replaced by Chinwe Nnanna, Cyber
Security & Machine Learning Expert. No link on the card — the other five carry none.

Skeletons. The two genuinely slow loads on this site are out of our hands: 110
product images hotlinked from vendor CDNs, and the Maps iframe. Both now reserve
their space and shimmer while they wait, and so do the cart and checkout line
items, which are painted from localStorage a frame or two after DOMContentLoaded.
The class goes on the container, never the <img> — an image with no bytes yet has
no box to paint into. Static under prefers-reduced-motion, hidden in print, and
content-visibility keeps the 100-odd off-screen skeletons from animating at all.

tools/apply-chrome.py. Steps 1-9 are the one-shot migration off the pre-redesign
markup; they ran in Phase 1.6b and their output is what is committed, so every run
since has died at step 1 with "nav matched 0 times" before reaching cart.html and
checkout.html — `git checkout products.html` does not help, because git holds the
migrated file too. The migration is now guarded behind a detection of its own
output, and the blocks that must keep re-applying — head, sprite, announcement,
nav, hero, CTA, footer, card template — find themselves by the comment banners
they were written under. The script is idempotent again: two consecutive runs
produce byte-identical files.
